          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not necessarily, but they have more components that wear: hinge fasteners can loosen, gas struts and hydraulic assists weaken over the years, and the latch is a safety part that must engage cleanly. Regular bolt checks and attention to the lift assist keep a good folding treadmill reliable.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on a folding treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, on the right one. Choose a folding model with a full-length deck — around 60 inches is the usual guide for running — a motor with adequate continuous rating, and a sturdy frame. Many compact folding treadmills are built for walking and are less suited to regular running.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are non-folding treadmills worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If the treadmill can stay in place, yes. None of the budget goes on a hinge, lift assist or lighter construction, so you usually get a stiffer frame, longer deck and stronger motor for the same money, with fewer parts to maintain.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
